<SiteLayout title="5-Year Pay Revision Demand: 8th Pay Commission" description="Unions and pensioner groups want pay revised every 5 years instead of 10, or a permanent pay review body. See who asked, what else was demanded and the status." slug="8th-pay-commission-five-year-pay-revision" h1="5-Year Pay Revision Demand to the 8th Pay Commission" lead="The demand to cut the 10-year gap, who made it and what is decided." faq={faq} toc={toc} related={related} pageType="article" breadcrumbLabel="Five-Year Pay Revision">

<div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Several employee and pensioner bodies have asked the 8th Pay Commission to move from a 10-year pay revision cycle to a 5-year cycle, or to create a permanent pay review body. It is a demand only. The commission has announced no decision.</p></div>

<h2 id="cycle">Today's 10-year pattern</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Commission</th><th>Revision applies from</th><th>Gap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">6th CPC</th><td>1 January 2006</td><td>10 years</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">7th CPC</th><td>1 January 2016</td><td>10 years</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">8th CPC (reference date reported)</th><td>1 January 2026</td><td>Not announced</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">If revised every 5 years (demand)</th><td>2021, 2026, 2031 and so on</td><td>5 years</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Between commissions, employees get Dearness Allowance twice a year and annual increments, and pensioners get Dearness Relief. A full restructuring of pay comes only with a new commission.</p>

<h2 id="who">Who has asked for it</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bharat Pensioners Samaj</strong> included a five-year revision cycle among 13 major proposals in a memorandum dated 7 August 2026.</li>
<li><strong>The staff side of the NC-JCM</strong> has also pointed to the need for more frequent revisions.</li>
<li><strong>The Railway Senior Citizens Welfare Society</strong> has raised the effect of long gaps on purchasing power.</li>
<li><strong>The Federation of National Postal Organisations</strong> has proposed a permanent pay review body instead of a new commission every decade.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="other">Other proposals in the same memoranda</h2>
<ul>
<li>Quarterly revision of DA and DR based on a three-month average of the index.</li>
<li>Merging DA with basic pension once it crosses 25%.</li>
<li>Fuller compensation for inflation for pensioners.</li>
</ul>
<p>None of these has been approved. See the full list of <a href="/8th-pay-commission-demands-interim-relief/">staff demands</a>.</p>

<h2 id="arguments">Why the idea matters</h2>
<p>Supporters say prices, rent and health costs rise faster than DA can cover over ten years. The counter-argument usually raised is the cost to the exchequer and the administrative burden of frequent revisions. The commission will weigh these when it reports, which is due around May 2027.</p>
<p>For the timeline, read the <a href="/8th-pay-commission-latest-news/">latest news</a> and <a href="/8th-pay-commission-consultations/">consultations</a>.</p>

</SiteLayout>
